<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The rest of Info.plist is generated from build settings
	     (GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE). What lives here is the structured keys, which
	     cannot be build settings at all, and the prose ones, which can but are
	     unreadable when they are: an INFOPLIST_KEY_… line in the project file is
	     a sentence nobody will ever find again. -->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<!-- Not NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. This permits plain http only to local
		     names — a server at 192.168.x.x or hexplore.local, which is exactly
		     how this app is self-hosted on a home network. Anything on the
		     public internet still has to be HTTPS, so putting the map behind
		     `tailscale serve` remains the right way to reach it from outside. -->
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HexPlore connects to the server you run on your own network.</string>
	<!-- Three location keys, because macOS has changed its mind about which one
	     it reads. `NSLocationUsageDescription` is the long-standing Mac key;
	     the two iOS-shaped ones are honoured from macOS 11 onwards and are what
	     a modern `requestAlwaysAuthorization` looks for. Missing the one in use
	     does not warn — the prompt simply never appears and location silently
	     never works, so all three are present and say the same thing.

	     Note there is no "While Using the App" state on macOS to write a
	     separate sentence for: the system has one location grant. See
	     LocationLogger.apply(). -->
	<key>NSLocationUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HexPlore uses your location to show where you are on the map, and — if you turn it on — to fill in the places this Mac has been.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HexPlore uses your location to show where you are on the map.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HexPlore fills in your map with the places you go while it is running. Everything it records is sent to the server you run, and nowhere else.</string>
	<!-- Read-only. The scan reads metadata alone — the coordinate a photo
	     carries and the date it was taken — and an image is opened only when you
	     click one on the map, to show it to you here. Nothing is written, and no
	     image is ever uploaded. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HexPlore reads the location a photo was taken at, so the places you have photographed appear on your map. Clicking one of those places shows the photo itself, on this Mac — no image is ever uploaded.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
